  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nWe address the Erdos minimum overlap problem by using a multi-resolution optimization strategy combined with Bayesian optimization and adaptive interpolation. The core idea is to first find promising patterns in a coarse grid (64 points) using Bayesian optimization, then refine these patterns at higher resolutions (128 and 256 points) using local optimization. This leverages both exploration (Bayesian optimization for global structure) and exploitation (gradient-based refinement). The algorithm adapts its grid resolutions based on a time budget, prioritizing coarser grids to escape local optima while maintaining focus on promising candidates throughout the optimization process.\n\nMathematical specification\nVariables: h(x) \u2208 [0,1] with \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1  \nObjective: C5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx (modeled via discrete correlation)  \nConstraints: sum(h) = n_points / 2 (box [0,1] constraints enforced via projection)  \n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Parse input:\n   - global parent (if available) at 256 points\n   - random seed and budget time\n\n2. Grid resolution stages (sample sizes: 64, 128, 256) with adaptive iteration budgets:\n   max_iter_64 = 1000 (if within budget)\n   max_iter_128 = 1000\n   max_iter_256 = 1000\n\n   For each stage:\n   a. Extract candidates from previous stage via bilinear interpolation with projection to constraints\n   b. Use Bayesian optimization (scipy.optimize) to find candidate vectors with low max_correlation\n   c. For each candidate in Bayesian batch:\n        Compute score via np.correlate(..., mode=\"full\") * dx\n        Update global best if lower score found\n   d. Maintain active candidate set with Pareto frontier (lower score)\n\n3. Candidate evaluation:\n   For grid of size `n`:\n      - Interpolate from parent (if available) or uniform start\n      - Project to sum(n/2) and [0,1] box\n      - Compute max_correlation via np.correlate(..., mode=\"full\") * (2.0/n)\n      - Accept candidate with score improvement\n\n4. Resolution transfers:\n   For stage \u2192 stage+1:\n      - Sample active candidates uniformly\n      - Upsample via linear interpolation with projection (sum preservation)\n      - Clip values [0,1] if needed\n\n5. Local refinement:\n   For final candidate set:\n      - Apply projected gradient descent to fine-tune\n      - Ensure sum constraint via projection at each iteration\n      - Use adaptive step sizes based on correlation gradient\n\nSearch choices and variations\n- Primary resolution: 256 points if parent exists \n- Active methods:\n   * Bayesian optimization with default parameters\n   * Gradient descent with adaptive steps\n   * Grid doubling with interpolation\n- Adaptive components:\n   * Spatial interpolation factor: parent_grid/2^k\n   * Score threshold: improve over current_best score (relaxation factor 0.95)\n   * Budget adaptation: min(assigned_budget, remaining_time)\n\nNumerical robustness and memory\n- Track active_candidates only (store scores and vector pointers)\n- Project to constraints via: clip \u2192 scale \u2192 cumsum adjustment\n- Graceful degradation:\n   * Fallback to uniform array if no bounds met\n   * Fallback interpolation if project fails\n\nRuntimesafeguardsaandreturncontract\n- Time monitoring at start and per-batch\n- Early termination if time exceeds budget: return current_best candidate\n- Emergency fallback: if no valid candidate after 50% budget, return interpolated parent\n- Exits cleanly even on numerical instability\n- Uses scipy optimize.minimize with L-BFGS-B for Bayesian optimization\n\nReturn: (best_h, best_score, n_points=256)\n\nDocuments all stages to ensure self-containment and reproducibility.",
